---
// **This import's position is load-bearing for the bytes of `/contact`.** Measured:
//
//   this file as it is now ..................... <style>, TrialForm, BaseLayout
//   any *new* module imported below TrialForm ... TrialForm, <style>, BaseLayout
//   a module already in the graph, below it ..... unchanged
//
// Astro orders a page's stylesheets by the order it crawls the page's imports. This
// component's own styles are inlined and `TrialForm`'s are an external `<link>`, so
// introducing a module to the graph below the `TrialForm` import moves the inlined block
// past that `<link>`. Both are scoped, so nothing renders differently — but MUSE-20 is a
// migration whose acceptance test is a byte-for-byte `diff -r` against the previous
// deploy, and a moved `<style>` is a byte.
//
// Which positions are safe is a property of the individual file, not a rule: `Home.astro`
// has the same exposure and tolerates the import *nowhere* (three placements tried, three
// identical diffs), which is why it takes the address as a prop instead. If you add an
// import to a component that renders `TrialForm`, build and `diff -r`.
//
// What *is* now a guard rather than a comment: `test/cascade.test.ts` (MUSE-43) pins the
// global layer to the front of every page's `<head>` and names the component whose
// imports moved. It does not pin two scoped sheets against each other — they cannot tie,
// so swapping them changes bytes and nothing else — so the paragraph above is still the
// only thing watching that, and it is still only a comment.
import { getSiteSettings, socialUrl } from '../lib/sanity';
import TrialForm from './TrialForm.astro';
// Below the `TrialForm` import deliberately, and safe for the reason the table above
// gives: `../lib/forms` is already in this page's import graph (`TrialForm` imports it),
// so it adds no stylesheet and moves nothing. A *new* module here would.
import { FORM_HAS_PROCESSOR } from '../lib/forms';
import { localeUrl, type Locale } from '../lib/i18n';
import { STUDIO } from '../lib/nav';

/**
 * `/contact` — the same trial form as `/#trial`, given a page of its own.
 *
 * It exists because the nav's Contact entry has pointed at a 404 since the rebuild, and
 * because a link someone can send a friend ("prijavi se ovdje") should not be a fragment
 * halfway down the homepage.
 */
interface Props {
  locale: Locale;
}
const { locale } = Astro.props;

/**
 * Contact details are content (MUSE-20), the address included — rendered whole here, and
 * split into two lines in the footer. Only the maps link is still code; the schema has no
 * field for it.
 */
const settings = await getSiteSettings();

/**
 * **The hero's instruction is chosen by the form's own configuration** (MUSE-84).
 *
 * The lede told the visitor to fill the form in — on a deploy where
 * `PUBLIC_FORM_ENDPOINT` is unset, so `TrialForm.astro` throws before any `fetch` and
 * every submission ends by offering the studio inbox instead (MUSE-12 records that the
 * form has never delivered a message). The instruction was false at the moment it was
 * followed, and `/privacy` — corrected in MUSE-72, gated on this same constant — said the
 * opposite about the same form on the same deploy.
 *
 * So there is no third state and no new fact: `FORM_HAS_PROCESSOR` is the expression the
 * form's script tests before it will send, and the hero reads it. With an endpoint the
 * instruction is true and is restored; without one the honest instruction is the inbox,
 * which is what the form itself falls back to. The sentence ends in a colon and the
 * address follows it as a link — `FormCopy['noscript']`'s convention, for the same reason:
 * telling somebody to write to us without the address in front of them is half a fix.
 *
 * **„bez obveze" / "no commitment" is gone, nothing replaced it, and nothing guards it**
 * (MUSE-84's „also in scope"). „Bez partnera, bez iskustva" are facts about how a class
 * runs and the site says them everywhere, `siteSettings.tagline` included. „Bez obveze"
 * is a *term of an offer* — nobody has said what attending a first class commits you to,
 * and `/pricing` publishes three rates and no trial rate. Retiring an unsupported
 * commercial term needs no decision from the studio; adding one does (MUSE-71).
 *
 * **Nothing in the suite refuses it, and this comment used to say something did**
 * (MUSE-114). `test/pricing.test.ts` was credited here with refusing the English half „one
 * layer down", and it does not: its needle runs over `periodName(period, locale)` — the
 * three `PRICE_PERIODS` names, never page copy — and it is English-only, so the Croatian
 * would pass it even inside a period name. `RETIRED_CLAIMS` in
 * `test/helpers/offer-claims.ts` is where a guard would live and it has no entry for this
 * either, so the sentence can come back to this lede with every gate green; what stops it
 * is this paragraph. Accepted rather than fixed in passing, because the needle would have
 * to be a *class* rather than a literal (MUSE-84, MUSE-109) and an entry there carries the
 * live strings it was measured against — its own ticket, not a comment correction.
 *
 * **`elseLede` names no mechanism, and that is what makes it ungated** (MUSE-117).
 *
 * It used to open with a **conditional-inconvenience frame** — *if the form is too much
 * trouble for you, write to us instead* — four lines under a hero that had just said the
 * form cannot be received. A frame of that shape presupposes the mechanism works and
 * offers email to whoever finds it inconvenient; with no endpoint email is the only path
 * and convenience is not the question. The frame is gone and the rest of the sentence is
 * untouched, so the section still does its job — these are the direct channels, and the
 * list below it is what they are.
 *
 * Note what this comment does *not* do — spell the retired sentence, even to explain it.
 * `test/offerclaims.test.ts` reads everything under `src/` as plain text, so a comment
 * here is the first false positive any future needle for this frame would hit; that is
 * MUSE-71's rule and there is no exemption list. `docs/decisions/pages.md` holds the
 * verbatim before and after, and `test/trialform.test.ts` holds it as a tooth — neither
 * tree is scanned.
 *
 * It is **not** a third branch beside `lede`. A sentence that mentions no mechanism is
 * true in both configurations, which the `page` description has to be for a reason it
 * cannot escape (MUSE-84) and which this sentence can be for free — and a second
 * conditional is a second thing to keep agreeing with the form's own configuration.
 * `test/trialform.test.ts`'s `UNSENDABLE` is the guard: on a build with no endpoint,
 * every sentence of every form page that names the form has to say the form cannot be
 * sent. This one does not name it at all, so it passes in both states rather than by
 * branching.
 */
const t = {
  hr: {
    // MUSE-71: the eyebrow used to price the first class at nothing. It names the
    // subject of the hero instead, which is what every other eyebrow on the site does.
    eyebrow: 'Prvi sat',
    h1: 'Dođi na probni sat',
    lede: FORM_HAS_PROCESSOR
      ? 'Bez partnera, bez iskustva. Ispuni obrazac i javljamo se s terminom koji ti odgovara.'
      : 'Bez partnera, bez iskustva. Obrazac trenutačno ne možemo primiti, pa nam prijavu pošalji e-mailom na:',
    formHeading: 'Prijavi se',
    elseHeading: 'Ili nam se javi direktno',
    elseLede: 'Piši nam ili nas pronađi na društvenim mrežama.',
    where: 'Gdje smo',
    openMaps: 'Otvori u Google Maps',
    schedule: 'Pogledaj raspored',
  },
  en: {
    eyebrow: 'First class',
    h1: 'Come to a trial class',
    lede: FORM_HAS_PROCESSOR
      ? 'No partner, no experience. Fill in the form and we will come back with a time that suits you.'
      : 'No partner, no experience. We cannot receive the form at the moment, so send your request by email to:',
    formHeading: 'Sign up',
    elseHeading: 'Or reach us directly',
    elseLede: 'Write to us or find us on social media.',
    where: 'Where we are',
    openMaps: 'Open in Google Maps',
    schedule: 'See the schedule',
  },
}[locale];
---

<section class="hero">
  <p class="eyebrow">{t.eyebrow}</p>
  <h1 class="display">{t.h1}</h1>
  {/*
    With no endpoint the lede ends in a colon and the address is the next thing read, so
    the visitor can act on the honest instruction without scrolling for it (MUSE-84). The
    same inbox the form's own failure block and `/privacy` offer, read from the same CMS
    field — one address, three surfaces, no literal.
  */}
  <p class="lede">
    {t.lede}
    {!FORM_HAS_PROCESSOR && (
      <a class="mail" href={`mailto:${settings.email}`}>{settings.email}</a>
    )}
  </p>
</section>

<section class="section sunken">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2 class="displayM">{t.formHeading}</h2>
    <TrialForm locale={locale} />
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">{t.elseHeading}</p>
    <h2 class="displayM">{settings.address}</h2>
    <p class="lede">{t.elseLede}</p>

    <ul class="links">
      <li><a class="link" href={`mailto:${settings.email}`}>{settings.email}</a></li>
      <li>
        <a class="link" href={socialUrl(settings, 'instagram')} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer me">
          Instagram
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a class="link" href={STUDIO.maps} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
          {t.openMaps}
        </a>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <a class="btn btnSecondary" href={localeUrl('/schedule', locale)}>{t.schedule}</a>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .hero {
    max-width: 1280px;
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding: var(--space-8) var(--space-5) var(--space-7);
  }
  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .hero {
      padding: var(--space-10) var(--space-7) var(--space-8);
    }
  }

  .section {
    padding: var(--space-8) var(--space-5);
  }
  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .section {
      padding: var(--space-10) var(--space-7);
    }
  }
  .sunken {
    background: var(--surface-sunken);
  }
  .wrap {
    max-width: 1280px;
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    font-family: var(--font-label);
    font-weight: 300;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.32em;
    /* Č Ć Ž Š carry marks above the cap line — 1.4 is the floor in Croatian (§4.6) */
    line-height: 1.4;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--accent);
    margin: 0;
    max-width: none;
  }

  .display {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(2.75rem, 9vw, 6rem);
    line-height: 0.95;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    text-wrap: balance;
    margin: var(--space-4) 0 0;
  }
  .displayM {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(2.125rem, 5vw, 3.75rem);
    line-height: 1.05;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    text-wrap: balance;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
  }

  /* Inline in the lede, so it takes the paragraph's own size and leading — the `.link`
     rule below is a 44px row in a list and would break the sentence it sits in. §11.3's
     44px is about tap targets and names the chrome controls; a link inside a sentence is
     what WCAG 2.5.8's inline exception covers, and `npm run a11y` runs every rule axe
     enables by default, so a disagreement is reported rather than argued here. */
  .mail {
    color: var(--accent);
    text-underline-offset: 4px;
  }

  .links {
    list-style: none;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
  }
  .link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: 44px;
    color: var(--accent);
    text-underline-offset: 4px;
  }

  .btn {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    min-height: 48px;
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
    padding-inline: var(--space-6);
    border-radius: var(--radius-full);
    border: 1px solid transparent;
    font-family: var(--font-label);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.28em;
    line-height: 1.4;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition:
      transform var(--dur-fast) var(--ease),
      filter var(--dur-fast) var(--ease);
  }
  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .btn {
      min-height: 56px;
    }
  }
  .btn:hover {
    transform: translateY(-1px);
    filter: brightness(1.06);
  }
  .btnSecondary {
    border-color: var(--accent);
    color: var(--accent);
  }
</style>
